      "writtenOn": "2024-03-29T16:44:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That `btif_rc_is_connected_peer()` may be sort of important in that it\u0027ll keep you from connecting A2DP on a device that may have connected AVRCP, then disconnected AVRCP in the time that you were waiting.\n\nFrom the looks of it, what you\u0027re basically trying to do is notify the AV state machine of an AVRCP connection event. So instead of avoiding it if new_avrcp is active, then maybe you want to check and see if the device is still connected with a call over to avrcp_service?\n\nUltimately, if we removed the dead \"old avrcp\" code, we would want to call over to new avrcp anyways.\n\ni.e.:\n```\n// Make a function to get connection status from new AVRCP\n// I did separate if blocks for the old and new way, but you could\n// probably join both and output the same error message\nif (is_new_avrcp_enabled() \u0026\u0026 bluetooth::avrcp::AvrcpService::Get()-\u003egetConnectionState(peer-\u003ePeerAddress()) !\u003d CONNECTED) {\n    log::error(\"AVRCP peer {} is not connected in new (only) avrcp\",\n               ADDRESS_TO_LOGGABLE_CSTR(peer-\u003ePeerAddress()));\n    return;\n} else if (!btif_rc_is_connected_peer(peer-\u003ePeerAddress())) {\n    log::error(\"AVRCP peer {} is not connected in old (dead code) AVRCP\",\n               ADDRESS_TO_LOGGABLE_CSTR(peer-\u003ePeerAddress()));\n    return;\n  }\n}\n```",
      "parentUuid": "090daa1a_d3406e8a",
